{"id":"cash-stipends-paid","title":"The account called “Stipends”","question":"Who is paid from the City’s stipends account, and how much of it can be followed to the person who received it?","finding":"The ledger charges $16.7 million to its “Stipends” account from fiscal 2019 to 2026; the payment file, which removes many payments to individuals, shows $11.6 million through fiscal 2027 to date. The largest payee is a bank: US Bank National Association received $7.7 million, $7.1 million of it from Economic & Workforce Development in fiscal 2021 to 2025. The file does not say who received the stipends that passed through the bank, how many people, or in what form. $2.4 million went to payees that read as private persons (not named here), most of it from Arts Commission. Payments peaked at $2.9 million in fiscal 2023 and were $516,923 in fiscal 2026.","how":"Payments in the Controller’s vendor-payment file whose sub-account is “Stipends”, added up by department and payee. A payee that reads as a private person is not named: such payees are added together for each department, with a count where there are ten or more. A payment is dated by its voucher number; one that belongs to a year before fiscal 2019 is shown as 2018. The ledger figure is the same sub-account in the Spending and Revenue table.","limits":"A stipend is a payment to someone for taking part, not a wage or a purchase. Paying many of them through a bank is an ordinary way to issue prepaid cards (the Controller’s audit of the Human Services Agency describes one such arrangement), and it is the publisher’s stated policy to remove payments to individuals from this file, which is why the ledger shows more than the payment file. The other side of that protection is that the public file cannot show how a bank-issued stipend programme was run. What would settle it: the programme’s roster of stipends issued (count, amount, date, eligibility test) and the bank’s statement of cards loaded and unspent balances returned. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"payee","t":"Payee","type":"org"},{"k":"kind","t":"Kind of payee","type":"text"},{"k":"paid","t":"Paid, fiscal 2019–2027","type":"usd"},{"k":"first","t":"First year","type":"year"},{"k":"last","t":"Latest year","type":"year"},{"k":"payees","t":"Payees added together","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"department":"Economic & Workforce Development","payee":"US Bank National Association","payeeKey":null,"kind":"bank","paid":7132268,"first":"2021","last":"2025","payees":null},{"department":"Arts Commission","payee":"Private persons (not named)","payeeKey":null,"kind":"private persons","paid":2163781,"first":"2018","last":"2027","payees":910},{"department":"Police","payee":"US Bank National Association","payeeKey":null,"kind":"bank","paid":516498,"first":"2024","last":"2026","payees":null},{"department":"Economic & Workforce Development","payee":"Economic Development on Third","payeeKey":"ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT ON THIRD","kind":"organization or firm","paid":237000,"first":"2025","last":"2025","payees":null},{"department":"Public Library","payee":"Private persons (not named)","payeeKey":null,"kind":"private persons","paid":234305,"first":"2018","last":"2027","payees":14},{"department":"Treasurer-Tax Collector","payee":"Single payment payees (as the file names them)","payeeKey":null,"kind":"pooled one-off payments","paid"